    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Logic is in the right unit.** The capacity computation lives in `ClinicServiceImpl.isCityCapacityWarningActive`, alongside the existing `CITY_OWNER_LIMIT` / `assertCityCapacity` logic, and reuses the same `countOwnersInCity` helper. The threshold is a named constant (`CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD`) mirroring the existing `CITY_OWNER_LIMIT`, and the interface gets a matching contract with a clear Javadoc. This is exactly where a maintainer would expect the rule to live.\n- **The V1 helper rename is a clean generalization.** `withBulkSignupWarning` \u2192 `withDerivedWarnings` correctly broadens the method's responsibility now that it sets two derived flags, and the Javadoc was updated to explain the per-response vs. per-city distinction. Threading it through all call sites (get/create/update/list) keeps derivation in a single place rather than scattering `setCapacityWarning` calls.\n- **The V2 change is consistent with V2's existing style.** V2 already set `bulkSignupWarning` inline in the `forEach` lambda (it can't share V1's private helper across controller classes), so adding `setCapacityWarning` in the same lambda matches the local idiom rather than introducing a new pattern.\n- **The OpenAPI addition** places `capacityWarning` next to `bulkSignupWarning` with a parallel `readOnly` boolean shape and a description matching the spec.\n\nOne thing a maintainer might revisit later (but not a reason to block this structurally sound change): `withDerivedWarnings` now recomputes `isBulkSignupWarningActive()` per owner in `listOwners`, whereas the old code hoisted that per-response value out of the loop \u2014 a minor efficiency point, and out of scope for this review.",
